---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Kicker from '../components/content/Kicker.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/convert/CtaBand.astro';

const title = 'About | PainToBuild';
const description = 'What PainToBuild is, the weekly evidence system behind it, and why the operator writes under a pseudonym instead of a named byline.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  about: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PainToBuild', url: 'https://paintobuild.com' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogImagePath="/open-graph/about.png">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <section class="wrap section prose">
    <Kicker>About</Kicker>
    <h1>About PainToBuild</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      I run a small, weekly research system that reads public founder conversations for
      recurring, evidence-backed pain. I write up what it finds, including the parts that
      don't prove what people want them to prove.
    </p>

    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      Every week, the system reads new Reddit, Hacker News and IndieHackers activity for people
      describing a real, recurring problem in their own words. Candidates that reach at least
      three independent, first-person signals move into deeper validation: repetition across
      threads and platforms, evidence of money already moving nearby, and a read on how
      crowded the space already is. Everything that clears that bar gets logged, dated, and
      sourced in an evidence file before it becomes a page here or an entry in the $19 Validation
      Brief. Nothing gets published that isn't already evidenced in that file. See the
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> for the full rules.
    </p>

    <h2>Two research tracks</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Founder Solutions</strong> is the original track: validation research for
      early-stage founders and solopreneurs, covering recurring customer pain, buyers who can
      actually be reached, and a handful of practical tests before anyone builds anything.
      <strong>Everyday Default Audits</strong> is a newer, second track applying the same evidence
      standard outside of building a company: the productivity systems, communication habits and
      consumer "best practices" that get repeated as default advice but haven't been tested
      against real evidence. It's still early. See <a href="/everyday/">what's coming</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What this deliberately isn't</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not an idea generator, and not a community.</li>
      <li>Not an AI tool that scores your idea in thirty seconds.</li>
      <li>Not proof that anyone will pay for anything. Recurring pain and willingness to pay are
        different questions, and this site keeps them separate.</li>
      <li>Not built from posting or engaging on Reddit. The discovery process is read-only
        listening.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why no name</h2>
    <p>
      This work is deliberately separated from my unrelated day-job professional identity. That's
      not a marketing device, and it isn't meant to manufacture mystery. It's a boundary I keep on
      purpose, the same way plenty of people keep a side project separate from their employer.
      What earns trust here is whether the methodology is transparent enough to check, whether the
      evidence is dated and sourced, and whether the site is clear about what it hasn't proven.
      Judge PainToBuild on those, not on a name.
    </p>

    <p><a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a> if you have a question, a correction, or a signal to look into.</p>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="See the evidence standard"
    body="The independence rules, confidence tiers and decision criteria behind every page on this site."
    ctaHref="/methodology/"
    ctaLabel="Read the methodology"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section { padding-block: var(--s7) var(--s8); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--t-lede); color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
